00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:09.820
So this project is to provide aggregate allele
frequencies from dbGaP, and our goal is to

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.570
provide data from over 1 million subjects.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:24.140
I will use this as an introduction to dbGaP,
this dbGaP page.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.830
So dbGaP is a database of genotypes and phenotypes.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:35.810
It stores studies, thousands of studies, and
the result of looking at the association between

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:41.500
genotype and phenotype and effect on of course
the genes and everything else.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:49.170
It also has thousands of phenotype data sets
and hundreds of thousands of variables and

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.120
millions of subjects.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:58.670
And it also contains a lot of molecular data
set including all the genotyping so this shows

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:05.890
you that there are millions of subjects that
have been genotyped on various platforms including

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:11.220
micro arrays and direct sequencing from exome
and whole genome.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:21.620
So this project is to provide, aggregate all
this heterogeneous data from diverse platforms

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.420
and then homogenize and normalize and put
it in a standard format.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:32.300
And then data will be integrated to dbSNP
release and then be available through various

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.300
methods.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:41.270
If you already use dbSNP this should be very
little change to your current pipelines.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:49.750
So this is a brief summary of the data flow
so again we select approved study in the dbGaP

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:57.160
and then the QC process and then we compute
and analyze the subject and their ancestry

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:00.690
and assign them to a population.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:05.180
All the data from the various platforms are
converted into a standard VCF format.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:11.520
Then go through a computational pipeline,
normalize the variants, aggregate and compute

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.160
the frequency and then provide the products.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.480
I will not go into the detail how the variants
are normalized but just to say that this has

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:27.250
led to improvement in terms of the RS assignment
and clustering and reduce a lot of the issues

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:30.470
in the previous builds where we had duplicate
RefSNP and things like that.This normalization

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:44.730
has been published and there is also a webinar
from about a year ago, linked on the project

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.930
home page.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:51.770
So we had our first release about a month
ago.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:58.950
And just starting with a modest 42 studies
and that included about 100,000 subjects,

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:06.569
half a trillion genotypes and the output is
that this clusters down to about half 1 billion

00:04:06.569 --> 00:04:15.440
unique variants and most of those already
existed in dbSNP but we also found some novel

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.440
variants.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:22.830
This is just comparing with various projects
that had been released before.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:27.960
ALFA is comparable in terms of scale and scope
with other projects such as the gnomAD and

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:33.749
TOPMED and ExAC in terms of the number of
subjects and variants, okay?

00:04:33.749 --> 00:04:38.680
This is showing the breakdown in terms of
populations from the initial release.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:47.939
It is still biased or primarily European but
we do have some numbers for the continental

00:04:47.939 --> 00:04:50.440
larger populations.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:58.800
But our future plan is to improve and increase
the number of these subjects of the underrepresented

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.539
populations.

00:05:00.539 --> 00:05:06.520
But again initially we had what hundred thousand
subjects.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:13.059
But this is just a small fraction of the available
subjects in dbGaP so there is almost 2 and

00:05:13.059 --> 00:05:22.880
a half million subjects that have genotypes
and almost 1,000,000 1/2 subjects which are

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:29.990
possible candidates for the ALFA project so
look for more data coming.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:36.190
We also looked at comparing the data from
our project with the other projects and we

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:44.339
see the stack in terms of concordance, quality,
and in terms of the ancestry and the variants

00:05:44.339 --> 00:05:45.400
in the population.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:50.749
So here what we are looking at is compare
the ALFA data, the minor allele frequency,

00:05:50.749 --> 00:05:52.210
versus thousand genome project.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:58.880
so the 1000 genome minor allele frequency
is plotted on the X axis and alpha is on the

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.889
